-        <div class="lesson"><span class="label">Finding</span>ΔP was strongly influenced by albedo, aerosols and the Ångström exponent, which undermines the O₂ proxy correction. A model using MethaneSAT's own albedo fit better across scenes than one using VIIRS data alone, likely because the two satellites pass over about 90 minutes apart. After months on this approach I concluded that a regression could not separate aerosol effects from albedo effects without knowing the true CO₂ over each scene. That pointed me toward physics-based modeling.</div>
+        <details>
+          <summary>Methods</summary>
+          <p>MethaneSAT infers surface pressure from the oxygen absorption band at 1286 nm. Because O₂ is evenly mixed in the atmosphere, any gap between the retrieved pressure and the expected pressure (ΔP) should come from light-path errors such as aerosol scattering. I tested whether ΔP, combined with independent aerosol data from the VIIRS satellite, could be used in a regression that corrects the CO₂ retrieval.</p>
+          <ul class="points">
+            <li>Built automated R and Python pipelines to download, extract and align NetCDF data from two satellites with different resolutions, using the VIIRS 6 km Deep Blue aerosol product.</li>
+            <li>Matched observations in space and time (±90 minutes) across 100+ scenes.</li>
+            <li>Fit robust linear models of ΔP against aerosol optical depth, albedo (surface brightness) and the Ångström exponent (a proxy for aerosol particle size), using VIF (variance inflation factor) checks for multicollinearity and AICc (corrected Akaike information criterion) for model selection.</li>
+          </ul>
+          <p>Full finding: ΔP was strongly influenced by albedo, aerosols and the Ångström exponent, which undermines the O₂ proxy correction. A model using MethaneSAT's own albedo fit better across scenes than one using VIIRS data alone, likely because the two satellites pass over about 90 minutes apart. After months on this approach I concluded that a regression could not separate aerosol effects from albedo effects without knowing the true CO₂ over each scene.</p>
+        </details>
