-        <details open>
-          <summary>Abstract</summary>
+        <details>
+          <summary>Full abstract</summary>
           <blockquote>Accurate retrievals of greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions are essential to meaningfully informing policymakers on regional policies. However, the community has struggled to obtain retrievals of carbon dioxide (CO₂) with uncertainties below the real regional variations of gas concentration in the atmosphere at fine spatial scales. These variations are of only 1–3 ppm on a background of roughly 430 ppm, so the required accuracy is below 0.25%. The main source of uncertainty in CO₂ retrievals comes from aerosol scattering. Different aerosol types carry different scattering behaviors coherently with Mie scattering theory, which makes it difficult to correct their impact on greenhouse gas retrievals. To solve the problem, we explored the dependency of these biases on various aerosol parameters, surface reflectance and geometry by simulating satellite retrievals using the radiative transfer model vSmartMOM. We devised a pipeline that is able to estimate the impact of aerosols on the signal in simulated noise conditions similar to those of MethaneSAT, a high resolution GHG sensing satellite, by using the fact that atmospheric parameters can be considered roughly uniform over small areas, whereas the surface reflectance underneath is sharply varying. On 88 simulated test scenes, the best correction reduces the aerosol-induced CO₂ bias from a median of 9.89 ppm to 0.34 ppm (0.079%), within the 0.25% requirement, although its root mean squared error of 1.25 ppm (0.29%) remains just above it. These studies provide a basis for current and future high resolution GHG remote sensing missions to successfully quantify and correct aerosol-scattering-induced bias, thus retrieving more accurate estimates of GHG concentrations.</blockquote>
         </details>
